\section{What it costs, and what \code{n} to use}{

A refit is a whole fit: the frame, the tape and the optimization
again. One call therefore costs \code{n} fits plus one \code{sdreport()}, which
is paid once for the jitter scale and is the same one \code{summary()}
would have paid.

Measured at the 25 000-row design of \code{dev/scale-findings.md} (50
sequences of 500, \code{K = 3} gaussian, \code{tr12 ~ (1 | id)}), alone on the
machine, three blocks of the same work from the same seed: one refit
is \strong{175.7 s and 26.5 gradient evaluations}, against 183.5 s and 16
gradient evaluations for the plain fit it started from, and the
\code{sdreport()} is 37.2 s. So \code{n = 4} is 12.3 minutes there, \code{n = 8} is
24.0 and \code{n = 16} is 47.5. The instrument's control (the largest
block over the smallest) reported 1.023 and the gradient counts were
identical across blocks, which is the check a clock cannot make.
\verb{dev/latent-2p3-starts-cost.R} is the script.

Twenty-four minutes is not something to do by accident on a model
that took three to fit, so on anything that size set \code{n} from the
\code{seconds} line of a first, small call rather than from the default.

\code{n} cannot have a default that is right at every size, and the one
here is a starting point rather than a recommendation. What it is
chosen from: on the probe in \code{dev/hmm-feasibility.md} a single
jittered refit at \code{jitter = 2} reaches the better optimum about 6
times in 10, so \code{n = 8} misses it with probability about 4e-4 and
\code{n = 4} with probability about 2e-2. That rate is a property of that
surface, not a constant, so treat \code{n} as something to raise when the
printed summary shows more than one optimum and to price from the
\code{seconds} line when the model is large.
}

\section{Where the starts come from}{

Each start is the fit's own estimates plus a normal draw per outer
parameter, with standard deviation \code{jitter} times that parameter's
standard error. Standard errors make \code{jitter} a distance in the
answer rather than in an internal coordinate. Where the fit reports
no usable standard error, the median of the ones it does report is
used, and where it reports none at all every parameter is moved by
\code{jitter} on the internal scale; the printed summary names which of
the three applied. Random effects start from the original fit's
conditional modes, since the inner problem is re-solved either way.
}

